# ThreadSanitizer suppressions.
#
# Usage:
#   TSAN_OPTIONS="suppressions=$PWD/tsan.supp report_mutex_bugs=0" \
#     setarch $(uname -m) -R ./build/tsan/src/app/minecraft --render-distance 6 \
#       --capture /tmp/shot.ppm
#
# (setarch -R is needed on this kernel; see docs/HANDOFF.md.)
#
# `report_mutex_bugs=0` is for the *application* run only. GTK's accessibility stack
# unlocks mutexes it does not hold, in a different library each time, so suppressing
# them by name is whack-a-mole. Engine code uses std::lock_guard exclusively and
# cannot produce that class of report, so switching the checker off costs nothing
# here. The unit-test run links no GTK and keeps every check enabled -- which is the
# run that actually exercises MpmcQueue and JobSystem.
#
# Everything here is third-party and unreachable from engine code. GLFW's Wayland
# backend pulls in GTK, which pulls in glib, gio, gobject, fontconfig and pango, and
# those race internally on their own refcounts and caches during window creation.
# Engine frames appear in those stacks only as the caller of glfwCreateWindow.
#
# This file exists because leaving them unsuppressed defeats the purpose of running
# TSan at all: a real race in the streaming pipeline would be one report among thirty
# that are always there, and would simply be skimmed past.
#
# Add nothing here for engine code. If mc:: appears as a racing access rather than as
# a caller, that is a bug to fix, not to suppress.

called_from_lib:libglib-2.0.so
called_from_lib:libgio-2.0.so
called_from_lib:libgobject-2.0.so
called_from_lib:libgmodule-2.0.so
called_from_lib:libgtk-3.so
called_from_lib:libgdk-3.so
called_from_lib:libgdk_pixbuf-2.0.so
called_from_lib:libatk-1.0.so
called_from_lib:libfontconfig.so
called_from_lib:libpango-1.0.so
called_from_lib:libpangoft2-1.0.so
called_from_lib:libpangocairo-1.0.so
called_from_lib:libcairo.so
